<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Solicinium</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">368</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Solicinium was fought in 368 between the Roman army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-i">Valentinian I</a> and his young son Gratian and the Alemanni, at a hill site in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> whose location is uncertain. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rando">Rando</a>'s raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, Valentinian invaded Alemannic territory; the Alemanni defended a steep height but were driven off, with heavy losses on both sides.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-solicinium.jpg" alt="Battle of Solicinium" width="320" height="484" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wikid77 (; derivative work by of map from WP Geo_Swan, cropping &amp; enlarging labels) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neckar_watershed_closer.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the death of Julian in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 363, the Alamanni set aside the truce he had imposed after his campaigns beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in 357, 358, 359 and 360 and renewed their raids into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, complaining that the ministers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-i">Valentinian I</a> had failed to pay the customary tribute. They crossed the Rhine in 366 and returned with plunder. The next year they defeated Valentinian's generals in two battles and captured several standards, until Jovinus beat two of their detachments along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a> and routed their united forces at Chalons-Sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a>, with losses put at up to 10,000 against no more than 1,200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Early in 368 the chieftain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rando">Rando</a> fell without warning on Moguntiacum, put its defenceless inhabitants to the sword and withdrew over the river. Valentinian resolved to forestall further raids by a campaign in their own territory beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. Count Sebastian was sent to encircle the enemy from the south by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Rhaetia</a>, while the emperor advanced from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> with the forces of the west. The Alamanni, unable to defend their fields and villages, withdrew into the mountains and camped on a hill called Solicinium, in the area of Württemberg.</p><p>While reconnoitring their position, Valentinian was nearly captured by an advanced party lying in ambush, and lost his helmet and his standard-bearer as he retreated. Little is known of the battle itself. It appears that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> carried the height by a general assault up the slope, and that the Alamanni, driven from the summit, fled down the far side into the hands of Sebastian, who had been placed in their rear.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The site of the battle is unknown, and no archaeological evidence has been found. Proposed sites include Sulz am <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, Schwetzingen, Rottenburg (Sülchen), Glauberg and the Spitzberg near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a>, spread over an area roughly 200 km across. The most recent research places the battle in the north of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hechingen">Hechingen</a>, with Solicinium where the town's Roman museum now stands.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Solicinium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
